  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q3-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "One line passes through the points $(1,2)$ and $(2,3)$. Another line passes through the points $(1,5)$ and $(2,4)$. Find the point of intersection of these two lines.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\left(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2}\\right)$",
      "worked": "For the first line:\n$$\\begin{align*}m_1&=\\frac{3-2}{2-1}=1\\\\y-2&=x-1\\\\y&=x+1\\end{align*}$$\nFor the second line:\n$$\\begin{align*}m_2&=\\frac{4-5}{2-1}=-1\\\\y-5&=-(x-1)\\\\y&=-x+6\\end{align*}$$\nEquating the two expressions for $y$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x+1&=-x+6\\\\2x&=5\\\\x&=\\frac{5}{2}\\\\y&=\\frac{5}{2}+1\\\\y&=\\frac{7}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nThe point of intersection is $\\left(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2}\\right)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 130
  },
